        <p><strong>How do you become a FOH Audio Engineer?</strong><br>A: FOH Audio Engineering requires years of live sound experience, console training (Avid, Yamaha, Allen &amp; Heath, Midas), system tuning skills, and the ability to make real-time mix decisions under pressure. Most engineers start in monitor engineering or local crew roles.</p>

        <p><strong>Is a degree required to be a Tour Manager?</strong><br>A: A degree with a goal is overrated in live sound. You'll learn 10 times more from doing stuff than by studying stuff. Most successful tour managers started in local crew roles and worked their way up through hands-on experience.</p>

        <p><strong>What do you do if you're not sure about a technical decision?</strong><br>A: Test your limits, but don't be afraid to know where they are. Better to ask during soundcheck than to pretend you know and risk the show. I've seen engineers try to fake their way through things they don't understand, and it never ends well.</p>

